WEBVTT

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:22.240
Speaker 1: In 476 AD, the Western Roman Empire fell, its power scattered across Europe.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:25.930
Speaker 1: But as one empire crumbled, another was rip.

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:33.760
Speaker 1: What was this new force that would unite Europe, preserve knowledge and shape the future of Christianity?

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:41.040
Speaker 1: This is the story of how the Catholic Church became the backbone of European civilization.

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:49.280
Speaker 1: After the fall of Rome, the Catholic Church stepped in as a unifying force across Europe.

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:57.410
Speaker 1: Without a central political authority, the Church became the one institution capable of providing stability.

00:00:58.610 --> 00:01:00.450
Speaker 1: But how did it gain such power?

00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:04.530
Speaker 1: Monasteries played a crucial role.

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:13.010
Speaker 1: They preserved knowledge, copying texts from the ancient world and keeping learning alive through the Dark Ages.

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:18.540
Speaker 1: Without these monks, we might have lost much of what we know today.

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:26.540
Speaker 1: As Europe began to emerge from the shadows of Rome's fall, a new method of thinking emerged.

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:28.380
Speaker 1: Scholasticism.

00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:34.380
Speaker 1: This was a critical time when faith and reason began to collide.

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:37.900
Speaker 1: What if faith wasn't enough to explain the world?

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:42.780
Speaker 1: Could reason, too be a path to understanding God's creation?

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:51.660
Speaker 1: Scholars, influenced by the teachings of Aristotle and other ancient philosophers began to ask these big questions.

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:56.060
Speaker 1: Who were the minds behind this intellectual revolution?

00:01:56.940 --> 00:02:03.740
Speaker 1: None other than Thomas Aquinas, whose writings bridged the gap between faith and reason.

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:12.000
Speaker 1: In 800 AD, Charlemagne was crowned Holy Roman Emperor by Pope Leo III.

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:15.040
Speaker 1: This wasn't just a political event.

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:19.120
Speaker 1: It was a symbol of the growing power of the Church.

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:21.520
Speaker 1: But what did it mean for Europe?

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:30.640
Speaker 1: The Church and the Emperor would now rule side by side, a partnership that would shape European history for centuries to come.

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:34.160
Speaker 1: But could one man truly unite a continent?

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:41.660
Speaker 1: During this period, the Church faced not only external threats, but internal divisions.

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:47.900
Speaker 1: Heresies like Catharism and Waldensianism questioned the authority of the Church.

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:50.700
Speaker 1: What was the response of the Church?

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:52.300
Speaker 1: Inquisitions.

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:55.100
Speaker 1: An attempt to root out these beliefs.

00:02:55.820 --> 00:02:58.620
Speaker 1: But could this really preserve the church's unity?

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:11.450
Speaker 1: From 1096 to 1291, the Crusades saw thousands of Christian knights march into the Holy Land to reclaim Jerusalem from Muslim control.

00:03:12.410 --> 00:03:15.770
Speaker 1: But were these wars about faith or something else?

00:03:16.570 --> 00:03:18.970
Speaker 1: The Church believed these wars were holy.

00:03:19.130 --> 00:03:23.530
Speaker 1: But the impact on Europe and the Middle east were far from simple.

00:03:24.650 --> 00:03:31.040
Speaker 1: How did these campaigns shape relations between Christians and Muslims for centuries to come?

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:36.480
Speaker 1: In 1054 AD, a defining moment occurred.

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:38.080
Speaker 1: The Great Schism.

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:45.280
Speaker 1: What caused this split between the Eastern Orthodox Church and the Roman Catholic Church?

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:53.520
Speaker 1: Theological disputes like the filioque clause and disagreements over papal authority set the stage for the split.

00:03:54.890 --> 00:04:03.530
Speaker 1: The tension reached a breaking point when Patriarch Michael Cerularius and Pope Leo IX excommunicated each other.

00:04:04.490 --> 00:04:06.490
Speaker 1: Could this division be healed?

00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:09.130
Speaker 1: Or would it forever split the Christian world?

00:04:10.970 --> 00:04:21.160
Speaker 1: Fast forward to 1517, when a German monk named Martin Luther challenged the Catholic Church's practices with his 95 theses.

00:04:23.069 --> 00:04:25.789
Speaker 1: Why did his objections cause such an uproar?

00:04:26.269 --> 00:04:31.709
Speaker 1: It was the sale of indulgences, a practice Luther condemned as corrupt.

00:04:32.829 --> 00:04:42.269
Speaker 1: Luther's bold act sparked the Protestant Reformation, fracturing the church and leading to the rise of the new Christian denominations.

00:04:43.709 --> 00:04:52.280
Speaker 1: In response to the Protestant Reformation, the Catholic Church launched its own movement of renewal known as the Counter Reformation.

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:55.240
Speaker 1: What was the key to this revival?

00:04:55.960 --> 00:05:04.280
Speaker 1: The Council of Trent clarified Catholic doctrine and reformed the clergy.

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:14.280
Speaker 1: New religious orders, like the Jesuits founded by Ignatius of Loyola took on the mission of spreading Catholicism worldwide.

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:18.920
Speaker 1: Was the Counter Reformation truly about spiritual renewal?

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:22.440
Speaker 1: Or was it a battle for the soul of Christianity?

00:05:24.440 --> 00:05:39.080
Speaker 1: From the 16th to the 18th century, Christianity spread far beyond Europe's borders, tied to European exploration as explorers like Christopher Columbus and Vasco da Gama opened up new worlds.

00:05:39.320 --> 00:05:43.700
Speaker 1: Missionaries followed in Africa, Asia and the Americas.

00:05:45.140 --> 00:05:48.420
Speaker 1: But how would Christianity spread to the rest of the world?

00:05:49.060 --> 00:05:53.300
Speaker 1: How would it take root in Africa and later in Nigeria?

00:05:54.180 --> 00:06:01.140
Speaker 1: Let's continue the journey moving from the early days of the Church to the rise of Christianity in Africa.

00:06:03.540 --> 00:06:08.410
Speaker 1: Christianity's roots in Africa go far deeper than the colonial era.

00:06:09.130 --> 00:06:15.130
Speaker 1: In fact, Africa was home to some of the earliest encounters with the Christian faith.

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:24.890
Speaker 1: Long before missionaries arrived from Europe, Africa had already played a central role in the unfolding of Christianity.

00:06:25.850 --> 00:06:26.890
Speaker 1: How do we know this?

00:06:27.450 --> 00:06:30.170
Speaker 1: Well, the Bible itself provides us with clues.

00:06:31.930 --> 00:06:46.180
Speaker 1: The first significant African connection to Christianity happens when Joseph, Mary and the infant Jesus fled to Egypt to escape King Herod's decree to kill all male infants in Bethlehem.

00:06:47.780 --> 00:06:53.460
Speaker 1: This was the first significant biblical connection between Africa and the Christian faith.

00:06:53.540 --> 00:07:12.020
Speaker 1: As Matthew, chapter 2, verse 13 and Hosea 11:1 recount another African presence is in the story of the crucifixion of Jesus is Simon of Cyrene, a man from eastern Libya who was compelled to carry the cross of Jesus.

00:07:13.140 --> 00:07:21.220
Speaker 1: His story reminds us that Africa's connection to Christianity goes back to the very core of the Christian narrative.

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:28.500
Speaker 1: But perhaps the most remarkable African connection comes in the form of the Ethiopian eunuch.

00:07:29.630 --> 00:07:39.310
Speaker 1: In the Book of Acts, chapter 8, from verse 26 to verse 40, this African official encounters Philip, hears the gospel and is baptized.

00:07:40.350 --> 00:07:48.750
Speaker 1: This pivotal event marks one of the first documented instances of African conversion to Christianity.

00:07:49.550 --> 00:07:53.470
Speaker 1: Long before the widespread missions of the European colonial period.

00:07:55.160 --> 00:08:00.680
Speaker 1: By the third century, North Africa became a significant center for Christian thought.

00:08:01.640 --> 00:08:14.840
Speaker 1: Great theologians like Tertullian, Origen and Augustine, born in what we now call Egypt, Tunisia and Algeria, defended and spread Christianity across the region.

00:08:16.360 --> 00:08:21.320
Speaker 1: By the end of the 3rd century, Eastern Maghreb had a Christian majority.

00:08:22.260 --> 00:08:27.300
Speaker 1: Christianity spread far and wide From Egypt to the fringes of north Africa.

00:08:28.260 --> 00:08:31.140
Speaker 1: But this flourishing Was not to last forever.

00:08:31.940 --> 00:08:37.779
Speaker 1: By the 11th century, Islam began to replace Christianity in much of North Africa.

00:08:39.059 --> 00:08:49.580
Speaker 1: In 1317, a cathedral in dongola Was turned into a mosque, Signaling the end of a long Christian presence In the Sudanese region.

00:08:50.860 --> 00:08:57.900
Speaker 1: Christianity, once dominant, Became a minority In Egypt and the rest of the maghreb.

00:08:59.100 --> 00:09:02.620
Speaker 1: But there was one African kingdom Where Christianity thrived.

00:09:03.100 --> 00:09:03.980
Speaker 1: Ethiopia.

00:09:05.180 --> 00:09:13.500
Speaker 1: Dating Back to the 4th century, Ethiopia's Christian roots trace back to the conversion of king ezana by frumentius.

00:09:14.390 --> 00:09:17.750
Speaker 1: It remains one of the oldest Christian nations in the world.

00:09:18.790 --> 00:09:32.950
Speaker 1: Despite facing challenges from Islam, Ethiopia had maintained a unique Christian tradition Through the coptic church, its blend of Christianity and Hebrew practices distinguishing it from western forms.

00:09:34.310 --> 00:09:41.500
Speaker 1: From these early roots, Christianity in Africa flourished in some areas and faced a decline in others.

00:09:42.780 --> 00:09:50.460
Speaker 1: By the time the seventh century rolled around, the northern parts of Africa had largely fallen under Islamic rule.

00:09:50.860 --> 00:09:55.100
Speaker 1: But Christianity never disappeared from the continent entirely.

00:09:57.260 --> 00:10:05.260
Speaker 1: So how did Christianity navigate the complex history Of Africa's encounters With foreign powers and indigenous cultures?

00:10:06.290 --> 00:10:10.850
Speaker 1: Lets explore how African kingdoms Would encounter Christianity anew.

00:10:11.250 --> 00:10:13.570
Speaker 1: Starting with the Portuguese explorers.
